		<category>Tech</category>
		<category>Alex Gibney</category>
		<category>Bleecker Street</category>
		<category>Elon Musk</category>
		<category>entertainment</category>
		<category>Musk documentary</category>
		<category>reviews</category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/culture/2026/10/review-4-hour-musk-documentary-feels-like-two-different-films/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        Alex Gibney's provocative film is compelling but overlong, and there's not much new here for Ars readers.
                    </description>
                                                                <ns2:encoded>
                            &lt;p&gt;Elon Musk is a very familiar figure to Ars readers, given his massive influence (for better and worse) on the technology sector over the last 20 years. Oscar-winning director Alex Gibney's latest biographical documentary—simply titled &lt;em&gt;Musk&lt;/em&gt;—covers a lot of wel</ns2:encoded>
                                    
                                    <ns3:comments>52</ns3:comments>
                
                
                <ns4:content height="648" medium="image" type="image/jpeg" url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/musk3-1152x648-1791058712.jpg" width="1152">
<ns4:thumbnail height="500" url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/musk3-500x500.jpg" width="500" />
<ns4:credit>AP</ns4:credit></ns4:content>
            </item>
